<div class="page-head">
  <%# The PLOT page's head arrangement, exactly: Run Job in grid row 1, the
      secondary action in row 2 col 2, blurb in row 2 col 1 -- and the blurb LAST
      in the row, which is what the phone media query places by grid-row. %>
  <div class="page-head-row page-head-row--split page-head-row--stacked">
    <h1 class="section-title"><span class="gradient-text">STATUS</span> — pipeline and server telemetry</h1>
    <%= link_to "Run Job", new_job_path, class: "btn btn-primary head-action head-action--top" %>
    <%# One secondary action, the JSON page's single row-2 cell rather than a
        pair: this page reports the recorded signal, so the raw log it summarises
        is what you reach for to see the rows unwound. %>
    <%= link_to "EVENTS", events_path, class: "btn btn-secondary head-action head-action--below" %>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary head-action head-action--below"
            data-popup="ml-popup"
            title="ML / ranking constants — how candidates are ordered and gated (read-only)"
            aria-label="ML and ranking constants, read-only help">ML / RANKING</button>
    <p class="page-head-blurb">What this studio has produced and what the machine running it has been doing — the pipeline-use tally, the host's own CPU/memory/swap history, and the candidate-triage event log. Read-only, and drawn by <span class="mono">chopshop-bp</span>.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<%# Which rendering the operator is about to read, and why. A configured runner
    means the panels below are drawn by the service; none means their numbers are
    rendered by the page itself, which is a real answer rather than an error. %>
<div class="actions-row">
  <span class="status-pill <%= @status_runners.any? ? "status-pass" : "status-pending" %>"
        id="statusRunnerPill"><%= @status_runners.any? ? "#{@status_runners.first} → chopshop-bp" : "no blend-plot runner — HTML" %></span>
  <span class="tech-tag" id="statusRunnerNote">
    <% if @status_runners.any? %>
      charts and the log are POSTed to <span class="mono"><%= Job.runners[@status_runners.first] %>/digest</span>
      and <span class="mono">/log</span>
    <% else %>
      set <span class="mono">CHOPSHOP_RUNNER_BP_URL</span> and
      <span class="mono">CHOPSHOP_RUNNER_BP_KIND=bp</span> to have the service draw them
    <% end %>
  </span>
</div>

<%# #status-telemetry-strip
    The LEARNING SIGNAL in counts: how much has been RECORDED, per kind, before
    you read the rows themselves. This is what the footer's check-engine light
    leads here for -- the light's dot says whether there is a signal at all, and
    this says how much. Scoped to the signal on purpose: the produced-file counts
    that used to sit in this strip are the PIPELINE USE panel's, where the same
    numbers are drawn, and one count in two places is one count too many. %>
<div class="telemetry-strip" data-telemetry-strip>
  <div class="telemetry-stat">
    <span class="telemetry-num mono" data-telemetry-count="events"><%= @telemetry[:events] %></span>
    <span class="telemetry-label">events</span>
  </div>
  <% Event::KINDS.each do |kind| %>
    <div class="telemetry-stat">
      <span class="telemetry-num mono" data-telemetry-count="<%= kind %>"><%= @telemetry[:by_kind][kind] %></span>
      <span class="telemetry-label"><%= kind %></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>
  <span class="tech-tag telemetry-tag"><%= @telemetry[:events].zero? ? "no training signal yet" : "training signal recorded" %></span>
</div>

<% if @status_plot_error %>
  <%# The service is configured but refused or was unreachable: say so rather than
      render a blank panel, and fall back to the same HTML the unconfigured page
      uses -- the data must not disappear because the service did. %>
  <p class="jev-note is-bad">chopshop-bp could not draw the panels — <%= @status_plot_error %></p>
<% end %>

<div class="status-grid">
  <%# #status-use-panel
      PIPELINE USE — what the studio has produced, as six counts and the bar
      chart that draws them. It REPLACED the source → job → candidate hierarchy,
      which restated the jobs table in a shape no number could be read out of:
      these are the measurements the jobs table cannot show. The chart is the
      service's (`digest`, chart "bar"); the tiles are the SAME hash, so the
      drawing and the numbers beside it cannot disagree. BAR, not line: six
      unrelated totals in a line would imply a progression that does not exist. %>
  <section class="card status-panel" data-status-panel="use">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h3>Pipeline use</h3>
      <span class="tech-tag mono">owner-scoped</span>
    </div>
    <p class="jev-question">What this studio has produced: <span class="mono">jobs</span> runs filed, <span class="mono">generated</span> rasters the generation layer drew, <span class="mono">traced</span> candidate SVGs the trace stage produced, <span class="mono">proofs</span> finished print checks, <span class="mono">scrapbooked</span> artifacts kept, and <span class="mono">composited</span> arrangements assembled. Every count is read from the record that produced it — none is asked of a runner, so a service being down cannot turn a recorded file into a zero.</p>

    <div class="telemetry-strip" data-use-strip>
      <% @use_bars.each do |(label, key)| %>
        <div class="telemetry-stat">
          <span class="telemetry-num mono" data-use-count="<%= label %>"><%= @use[key] %></span>
          <span class="telemetry-label"><%= label %></span>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <% if @use_svg %>
      <div class="status-svg" data-status-svg="use"><%= @use_svg.html_safe %></div>
    <% else %>
      <p class="jev-note" data-status-fallback="use">
        <% if @use.values.sum.zero? %>
          Nothing produced yet — run a job and its generated rasters, traced candidates and proofs appear here.
        <% else %>
          Not drawn: no blend-plot runner is configured or the service did not answer. The counts above are the same numbers.
        <% end %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <%# #status-server-panel
      THE HOST, not the jobs: CPU, memory and swap over the last six hours, from
      HostSample's minute samples, with the newest reading as tiles and the moment
      it was taken in the head. The chart is the service's (`digest`, chart
      "line", one series per metric). Nothing is drawn when there is nothing to
      draw: no samples, or no runner, prints the numbers and says so rather than
      showing an empty axis. %>
  <section class="card status-panel" data-status-panel="server">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h3>Server</h3>
      <span class="tech-tag mono" data-host-sampled-at>
        <% if @host %>
          last sample <%= @host.captured_at.utc.strftime("%H:%M UTC") %>
        <% else %>
          no samples yet
        <% end %>
      </span>
    </div>
    <p class="jev-question">The machine this studio runs on, sampled once a minute: CPU, memory and swap read from the kernel and kept for a week. A percentage is measured BETWEEN two samples, so the first reading after a restart is absent rather than zero — and a box with no swap reports that, not 0%.</p>

    <% if @host %>
      <div class="telemetry-strip" data-host-strip>
        <div class="telemetry-stat">
          <span class="telemetry-num mono" data-host-value="cpu"><%= @host.cpu_pct ? "#{@host.cpu_pct}%" : "—" %></span>
          <span class="telemetry-label">cpu</span>
        </div>
        <div class="telemetry-stat">
          <span class="telemetry-num mono" data-host-value="memory"><%= status_host_size(@host.mem_used_mb) %> / <%= status_host_size(@host.mem_total_mb) %></span>
          <span class="telemetry-label">memory<%= " · #{@host.mem_pct}%" if @host.mem_pct %></span>
        </div>
        <div class="telemetry-stat">
          <span class="telemetry-num mono" data-host-value="swap">
            <% if @host.swap_total_mb.to_i.zero? %>
              none configured
            <% else %>
              <%= status_host_size(@host.swap_used_mb) %> / <%= status_host_size(@host.swap_total_mb) %>
            <% end %>
          </span>
          <span class="telemetry-label">swap<%= " · #{@host.swap_pct}%" if @host.swap_pct %></span>
        </div>
        <div class="telemetry-stat">
          <span class="telemetry-num mono" data-host-value="load"><%= @host.load1 ? @host.load1.round(2) : "—" %></span>
          <span class="telemetry-label">load 1m</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <% if @host_stale %>
      <%# An old reading is not a current one. The sampler lives in the queue
          worker (config/recurring.yml), so a stopped sampler is a fact worth
          naming rather than a number worth showing twice. %>
      <p class="jev-note is-bad" data-host-stale>
        <% if @host %>
          The sampler has stopped — the newest reading is from
          <%= @host.captured_at.utc.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M UTC") %>. Check that the
          <span class="mono">host_sample</span> recurring task is running.
        <% else %>
          No samples have ever been taken. The sampler is the
          <span class="mono">host_sample</span> recurring task in
          <span class="mono">config/recurring.yml</span>, run by the Solid Queue worker.
        <% end %>
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%# #status-host-gap-annotation
         A nil bucket (a gap in the chart) means the sampler missed ticks during
         that bucket. When a run was in-flight then, the gap is explained: the
         sampler runs on the same 'runs' queue as the job itself, so a long trace
         delays it. The chart shows a hole; this note says WHY. A gap with NO
         overlapping run is NOT annotated -- it stays an unexplained absence,
         which is the honest answer. %>
    <% run_interrupted_gaps = @host_gaps.select { |g| g[:run_interrupted] } %>
    <% if run_interrupted_gaps.any? %>
      <p class="jev-note" data-host-gap-annotation>
        Sampling was interrupted by a run during
        <%= run_interrupted_gaps.map { |g| "#{g[:from].strftime('%H:%M')}" }.join(', ') %> UTC.
        The sampler shares the <span class="mono">runs</span> queue with the pipeline, so a long
        trace delays it — the gaps in the chart are expected during heavy use.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <% if @host_svg %>
      <div class="status-svg" data-status-svg="server"><%= @host_svg.html_safe %></div>
    <% elsif @host && @host_data[:points].zero? %>
      <p class="jev-note" data-status-fallback="server">Readings exist but none cover a whole sample interval yet — the chart appears within a few minutes of the first sample.</p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="jev-note" data-status-fallback="server">
        <% if @host_data[:points].zero? %>
          No chart yet — the history starts with the first sample, and a percentage needs the one before it.
        <% else %>
          Not drawn: no blend-plot runner is configured or the service did not answer. The tiles above are the newest reading.
        <% end %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <%# #status-log-panel — one row per recorded human action, newest first: the
      training signal a later learned ranker reads. Unchanged by the panels
      around it, and the only panel that is still a timeline. %>
  <section class="card status-panel" data-status-panel="log">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h3>Event log</h3>
      <span class="tech-tag mono"><%= @log_spec["events"].size %> events</span>
    </div>
    <p class="jev-question">Every recorded action, newest first — the training signal a later learned ranker reads. The label is the artifact's checksum where it has one, and the position is its rank in the (hard, advisory, id) order it was acted on.</p>

    <% if @log_svg %>
      <div class="status-svg" data-status-svg="log"><%= @log_svg.html_safe %></div>
    <% else %>
      <div class="status-fallback" data-status-fallback="log">
        <% if @events.any? %>
          <ol class="status-log">
            <% @events.each do |event| %>
              <li class="status-event">
                <time class="status-time mono" datetime="<%= event.created_at&.iso8601 %>"><%= event.created_at&.iso8601 %></time>
                <span class="status-kind"><%= event.event %></span>
                <span class="status-label mono"><%= status_event_label(event) %></span>
                <% if event.pos %><span class="status-pos mono">#<%= event.pos %></span><% end %>
              </li>
            <% end %>
          </ol>
        <% else %>
          <p class="jev-note">Nothing recorded yet — a star, pick or delete appends a row here.</p>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </section>
</div>

<%# #status-ml-panel
    The THIRD panel: the pipeline's shipped ML/ranking constants — the ranker
    that orders and gates candidates, the bandit that picks the next bucket, the
    triage gate defaults, and where the learned files live. It belongs on THIS
    page rather than on one of its own because the signal it is trained on is
    the log above it, and the footer's check-engine light leads here.

    Read-only by design, the rule the FLUX and PLOT pages hold for the same
    reason: it reports what the pipeline IS configured with (transcribed into
    Telemetry::CONFIG, with the shipped values verbatim) and configures
    nothing. Full width rather than a grid cell — the groups read as columns of a
    config file, not as a panel beside the drawings. %>


<%# #status-ml-popup
    The ML / ranking constants, moved OUT of the page body and behind this
    button. It was a full-width card below the three drawn panels, where it read
    as a fourth panel of measurements; it is not a measurement at all. It is the
    read-only help document for how chopshop-svg orders and gates candidates,
    transcribed into Telemetry::CONFIG with the shipped values verbatim, and it
    configures nothing. The schema page already opens help this way
    (data-popup -> aether.js openPopup), so this adds no new behaviour.

    Every word and every datum below is the block that used to sit here -- only
    the container changed. The popup is wide because the groups read as columns
    of a config file. %> 
<div class="popup popup-wide" id="ml-popup" role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
     aria-labelledby="ml-popup-title" hidden>
  <div class="popup-panel popup-remediation">
    <header class="popup-head">
      <h2 id="ml-popup-title">ML / ranking <span class="tech-tag mono">read-only configuration</span></h2>
      <button type="button" class="popup-close" data-popup-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    </header>
    <div class="popup-body">
<p class="jev-question">The constants behind the order you are shown: <span class="mono">chopshop-svg</span>
    gates and ranks candidates from this signal, and a bandit decides which bucket to try next —
    it never picks a winner, and nothing on this page can change any of it.</p>

  <div class="telemetry-config" data-telemetry-config>
    <% @telemetry_config.each do |group| %>
      <section class="telemetry-group" data-telemetry-group="<%= group[:title].parameterize %>">
        <h4 class="telemetry-group-h"><%= group[:title] %></h4>
        <p class="jev-note"><%= group[:note] %></p>
        <dl class="help-notes">
          <% group[:entries].each do |entry| %>
            <dt><%= entry[:title] %></dt>
            <dd><%= telemetry_body(entry[:body]) %></dd>
          <% end %>
        </dl>
      </section>
    <% end %>
  </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
